<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROBE C2. C1's DISCRIMINATOR. Byte-for-byte the same model as bkm-chain.dmn
  except for where the two knowledgeRequirement edges live:

      C1: outer -> inner        on the BKM
          result -> outer       on the decision
      C2: result -> outer       on the decision
          result -> inner       on the decision   <<< the transitive edge
          outer carries NO knowledgeRequirement at all

  What this asks: is an engine satisfied by the TRANSITIVE CLOSURE of the call
  graph pinned on the top caller, rather than by the direct-call edges pinned on
  each caller?

  WHAT THE PAIR MEANS.
    C1 pass, C2 pass -> both spellings work; the emitter may choose, and should
      choose direct edges because they are what a modeler renders and what an
      SCC over the requirement graph needs to be correct.
    C1 pass, C2 fail -> direct edges are mandatory. Cleanest outcome.
    C1 fail, C2 pass -> the emitted edge set is a transitive closure, NOT a
      direct-call set, and section 6.3.9's SCC would be running over a graph that
      is not the graph the engine consumes. That would be a spec-level
      correction, not a fixture note.

  Read the two files together; neither answers the question alone.

  MEASURED 2026-07-31, and the answer is the cleanest of the three possible
  ones: C1 passes, C2 FAILS ON BOTH ENGINES.

    KIE 8.44  : ERROR [ERR_COMPILING_FEEL] Error compiling FEEL expression
                'inner(v) * 10' for name 'outer' on node 'outer': Unknown
                variable 'inner'. Validator AND build, i.e. compile time, and
                no case is ever evaluated.
    Camunda 8 : parses, then `result` evaluates to null in every case, with no
                message of any kind. Caught here only by the harness's own
                null-value rule.

  So a knowledgeRequirement pinned on the top-level caller does NOT put the
  callee's name into an intermediate BKM's FEEL scope. The engines consume the
  DIRECT-CALL edge set, not its transitive closure, and section 6.3.9's SCC is
  therefore running over the same graph the engine consumes. Wired
  HarnessMustFail on both legs.

  Note the asymmetry in HOW they fail, because it recurs across this whole
  probe set: KIE refuses at compile time and names the missing binding; Camunda
  silently answers null. Every "is the engine a backstop" question in Phase 5
  has that same shape.

-->
<definitions xmlns="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20191111/MODEL/" id="definitions_bkm_chain_flat" name="bkm_chain_flat" namespace="https://legalese.com/l4/dmn/bkm_chain_flat" exporter="hand">
  <businessKnowledgeModel id="bkm_inner" name="inner">
    <variable id="bkm_inner_var" name="inner" typeRef="number"/>
    <encapsulatedLogic id="bkm_inner_logic">
      <formalParameter id="bkm_inner_p1" name="v" typeRef="number"/>
      <literalExpression id="bkm_inner_lit">
        <text>v + 1</text>
      </literalExpression>
    </encapsulatedLogic>
  </businessKnowledgeModel>
  <businessKnowledgeModel id="bkm_outer" name="outer">
    <variable id="bkm_outer_var" name="outer" typeRef="number"/>
    <encapsulatedLogic id="bkm_outer_logic">
      <formalParameter id="bkm_outer_p1" name="v" typeRef="number"/>
      <literalExpression id="bkm_outer_lit">
        <text>inner(v) * 10</text>
      </literalExpression>
    </encapsulatedLogic>
  </businessKnowledgeModel>
  <inputData id="input_n" name="n">
    <variable id="input_n_var" name="n" typeRef="number"/>
  </inputData>
  <decision id="decision_result" name="result">
    <variable id="decision_result_var" name="result" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_result_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_result_outer">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#bkm_outer"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_result_inner">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#bkm_inner"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_result_lit">
      <text>outer(n)</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
</definitions>
